DEBUGGING FREEFORTH2
====================

A practical guide to finding and fixing bugs in FreeForth2, drawn
from hard-won experience during the x86-64 port.  These techniques
apply to both i386 (ff) and x86-64 (ff64).


PHILOSOPHY
----------

1. Smallest repro first.  Before examining assembly or theorizing,
   strip the failure to the fewest words that reproduce it.  The
   header swap bug was found because "1 2 "test" here 0 header ss"
   proved the swap — no need for help/needed/marker complexity.

2. Cross-platform comparison.  i386 ff is the reference.  Run the
   same code on both and compare.  When "1 2 "test" marker ss"
   showed ( 1 2 ) on i386 but ( 2 1 ) on ff64, it immediately
   identified marker/header as the divergence point.

3. Empirical before theoretical.  Don't puzzle through source code
   or theorize about flag state — not until you have a small repro
   and concrete evidence to inform the reasoning.  The ct=1 bug and
   the _parse DUP1/DROP1 bug both resisted manual analysis but
   yielded quickly to GDB and binary search.


TECHNIQUES
----------

ss and .s — show the data stack
................................

  ss prints the data stack in ( depth; val1 val2 ... ) format.
  It always shows at least 2 items, even when depth is 0 or 1.
  This is deliberate: seeing non-zero values below the stack floor
  means something underflowed.  On i386 the sub-floor items should
  be zeroes; non-zero is a red flag.

    1 2 3 ss cr ;       \ prints: ( 3; 1 2 3 )
    ss cr ;              \ prints: ( 0; 0 0 ) — floor items visible

  .s is Lavarenne's original — it always shows 9 items regardless
  of depth, deliberately exposing the region below the stack floor.
  Same principle: non-zero below the floor means underflow.  ss is
  the compact version for quick checks; .s is for deep inspection.


Z` macro — compile-time stack tracing
......................................

  Define  : Z` ss ;  (backtick name makes it a macro).  Then use Z
  (dropping the backtick) inside a word definition.  Because the
  definition name has a backtick but the usage omits it, the body
  executes at compile time during the word's compilation, showing
  the compile-time stack at that point:

    : Z` ss ;
    : _back >in@ 1- dup Z BEGIN tib <> drop Z WHILE ...

  This is useful for tracing compile-time state through backtick
  macros and boot definitions.  For runtime tracing, just put ss
  directly in the word — no macro needed.


EOF binary search — find which word crashes
...........................................

  Insert EOF at progressively later points in a loaded file.  EOF
  is a compile-time macro that skips to end of file — everything
  after it is ignored during compilation.  When it stops crashing,
  the culprit is the word just after the EOF.

    \ file loaded by needed:
      H@ @ execute
      swap dup>r       \ <-- works up to here
      EOF              \ <-- insert here; move later until it crashes
      rest of code...
    ;

  This found that dup>r (unbalanced, corrupting return stack) was
  where the help preamble first went wrong, which led to
  investigating the stack state entering the preamble, which led to
  the marker bug.


GDB — inspect generated code
.............................

  FreeForth compiles at runtime, so there are no symbol names for
  generated code.  But GDB can disassemble at any address:

    echo 'test-input' | gdb -batch -ex 'run' -ex 'x/30i $rip-40' ./ff64

  Useful commands:
    x/Ni addr               disassemble N instructions at addr
    x/4gx $rsp              show return stack (4 qwords)
    x/4gx $r15              show data stack memory (below TOS/NOS)
    info reg rbx rdx r15 rbp    TOS, NOS, stack ptr, HERE

  When RIP is a small number (like 0x9), execution jumped to a data
  value — check what constant or literal has that value.  The return
  stack shows where the bad call/jump came from.


see, dis, dump — three views of compiled code
..............................................

  FreeForth has three tools for inspecting compiled code.  They
  overlap but each has strengths where the others fail.

  see — native Forth disassembler.  Lazy-loaded on both platforms.
  Decodes FreeForth idioms and labels jump targets with word names:

    : _t 1 2 + ; see _t ;
    see dup` ;               \ backtick macros too

  Strengths: labels calls/jumps with dictionary names (call under`),
  no external tools needed.  Weaknesses: limited opcode coverage —
  shows ??? for unrecognized instructions (REX-prefixed ops, some
  SIB forms).  Backtick macros are short, so see works well; long :
  definitions can hit unknown opcodes.

  dis` — GDB-based disassembler.  Available after see loads (they
  live in the same file).  Attaches GDB to the running process and
  uses disass/r:

    : _t 1 2 + ; see _t ;   \ loads see.ff (which includes dis)
    dis _t ;                 \ now dis is available

  Strengths: decodes ALL x86 instructions (GDB knows every opcode),
  shows raw bytes alongside.  Weaknesses: no FreeForth word-name
  labels, requires GDB installed, output is noisier (GDB
  attach/detach messages).

  dump — raw hex dump.  Always available (boot word):

    dup` ' 32 dump ;

  Strengths: shows exact bytes with no interpretation — essential
  when see shows ??? and you need to manually decode, or when
  checking data layout (header bytes, string encoding, litcomma
  values).  Weaknesses: no disassembly — you must decode bytes by
  hand or cross-reference with an opcode table.

  When to use which:

    Quick look at a word's structure      see
    see shows ??? for some instructions   dis (GDB decodes everything)
    Crash site / generated code at addr   GDB x/Ni addr
    Check raw bytes or data layout        dump
    Compare i386 vs ff64 code gen         see on both (same format)
    Verify litcomma values / headers      dump

  Use see on i386 to understand how Lavarenne's compiler generates
  code for a pattern, then compare with ff64 output for the same
  pattern.


Compile-time ss binary search
..............................

  Place "ss ;" as anonymous blocks at various points in boot code.
  Rebuild.  The stack depth shown reveals where a compile-time stack
  leak starts.  Move the ss calls around to narrow to one line.

    \ in ff2.boot, during compilation:
    ss ;    \ check compile-time stack here
    : someDef ... ;
    ss ;    \ and here — did depth change?

  This found the _back compile-time stack leak in experiment 150.


Comment-out to confirm
......................

  When a specific word is suspected, comment it out (or replace
  with a no-op) and rebuild.  If the bug disappears, you've
  confirmed the source.  But be careful — removing a word can mask
  the bug while introducing a different one.


CASE STUDIES
------------

The header swap bug (exp 155)
.............................

  Symptom: "help dup" on ff64 crashed with SEGV inside the
  Lavarenne help preamble.

  Debugging journey:
  1. EOF binary search in ff.help preamble -> crash starts at dup>r
  2. But dup>r works fine in isolation -> problem is the stack state
     entering the preamble
  3. ss probes around [ 2>r needed 2r> _] -> stack is ( 1 2 )
     before needed, but ( 2 1 ) inside the preamble
  4. Cross-platform comparison confirms: i386 sees ( 1 2 ), ff64
     sees ( 2 1 )
  5. Z` instrumentation inside needed -> swap happens between >r
     and marker
  6. Minimal repro: "1 2 "test" marker ss" — ff64 shows ( 2 1 )
  7. Further reduction: "1 2 "x" here 0 header ss" — header itself
     swaps
  8. Examined _header_forth in ff64.asm — mov rbx,[r15+16] and
     mov rdx,[r15+24] were loading the wrong registers
  9. Fix: swap the two loads.  One line, found by reducing a complex
     help system crash to a 1-line repro.

  Time from first symptom to fix: ~45 minutes of structured
  reduction.  Most of that was narrowing, not theorizing.


The ct=1 bug (exp 038)
.......................

  Symptom: words crashed with jmp 0x9.

  What didn't work: Extensive manual analysis of SWAPbit state,
  flag preservation, and stack operations — all red herrings.

  What worked: One GDB session showing jmp 0x9 immediately revealed
  that the compile-time stack was corrupted by a constant value
  (ct=1 pushed as data, then executed as an address).

  Lesson: Always check the generated code first.


The _parse DUP1/DROP1 bug (exp 103)
....................................

  Symptom: IF/ELSE/THEN in : definitions could corrupt definitions
  250+ lines later.

  What didn't work: Trying to trace ELSE's stack management
  manually through multiple definitions.

  What worked: Comparing _parse entry code between i386 and ff64 —
  i386 did DUP1, ff64 did DROP1.  Every parse/lnparse call consumed
  one extra stack item, causing cumulative compile-time stack
  corruption.


WHAT DOESN'T WORK
-----------------

  Manual SWAPbit tracing.  Too many toggles and adjusters to track
  reliably in your head.  Use GDB or see to see the actual emitted
  bytes.

  Theorizing without evidence.  Don't spend time hypothesizing
  about flag preservation, register clobbering, or stack corruption
  without first looking at the generated machine code.

  Progressive test simplification alone.  Narrowing a crash by
  removing words from a test can help, but is slow and can lead to
  wrong conclusions (creating a "simplified" test that crashes for
  a different reason).


QUICK REFERENCE
---------------

  Technique                  When to use
  -------------------------  -----------------------------------------
  ss                         Quick runtime stack check
  Z` macro (Z without `)     Compile-time probes inside a definition
  EOF                        Binary search in a loaded file
  Cross-platform ss          Detect i386/ff64 behavioral divergence
  GDB x/Ni                   Inspect generated machine code at crash
  GDB + ff64.sym             Assembly labels in GDB (break _compiler)
  see word                   Disassemble with FreeForth word labels
  dis word                   GDB disassembly (decodes all opcodes)
  addr N dump                Raw hex bytes, data layout, litcomma vals
  Compile-time ss ;          Find compile-time stack leaks in boot


FASM SYMBOLS IN GDB (fas2gdb)
-----------------------------

  The fas2gdb tool converts FASM's .fas symbol dump into a minimal
  ELF that GDB can load, giving you symbolic names for every
  assembly label in ff64.asm — _compiler, _dup, _drop, _start, tib,
  dstack, SC, cond_jmp, and ~250 more.

  Build:

    make ff64.sym

  This runs fasm with -s to produce ff64.fas, then fas2gdb reads
  the .fas format (documented in FASM's TOOLS/FAS.TXT) and emits
  ff64.sym — a minimal ELF64 with a .symtab section.  It auto-
  detects the load base from the linked binary's .flat section.

  Use in GDB:

    echo 'test code ;' | gdb -batch \
      -ex 'file ff64' \
      -ex 'add-symbol-file ff64.sym' \
      -ex 'break _compiler' \
      -ex 'run' \
      -ex 'x/5i $rip' \
      -ex 'info symbol $rip' \
      ./ff64

  What this gives you:

    break _compiler          set breakpoint by label name
    x/5i _dup                disassemble a primitive by name
    info symbol 0x403a2b     resolve an address to label+offset
    info symbol $rip         name the current crash location

  In a crash backtrace, instead of:
    #2  0x0000000000403a11 in ?? ()
  you get:
    _semi_exec + 24
    _compiler.bt_exec + 2
    _catch + 31

  Note: this only covers assembly-level labels from ff64.asm.
  Words defined in Forth (ff64.boot, loaded .ff files) compile
  at runtime into codebuf — those addresses are past boot64_end
  and have no static symbols.  Use see/dis/int3 for those.

  For FULL symbol coverage (assembly + Forth words), use:

    make ff64-full.sym

  This boots ff64, dumps .hdrs (the dictionary), and pipes it
  through fas2gdb --hdrs.  Result: ~490 symbols including every
  Forth word — nearly double the ~250 assembly labels.

    make ff-full.sym         i386 equivalent (~494 symbols)

  The -full.sym file replaces .sym (same output name), so GDB
  usage is identical.  It requires a working binary (boot must
  succeed); use .fas-based .sym when debugging boot failures.
